# Verified command execution implementation plan

Source proposal: `docs/verified-command-execution-proposal.md`

Prerequisite plan: `docs/directed-acyclic-graph.todo`

Related contracts:

- `docs/test-runner-service-design.md`
- `docs/new-command-proposals.md`

## Goal

Replace every built-in and project-defined public command path with one
run-scoped, Cypress-like verified-command runner. Every invocation must have a
finite wall-clock deadline, an explicit read-only preflight gate, one declared
primary-execution policy, truthful intrinsic verification, optional caller
verification, stage-aware evidence, and effect-driven verified cleanup. The
same architecture must support suite-, test-, and service-owned cleanup,
run-wide resource claims, explicit dependency ordering, retained transaction
history, workflows, and proof-gated execution retry.

Preserve existing public call shapes and return values where the proposal
requires compatibility, but perform a complete definition-based conversion:
do not add a runtime adapter for callback-only project commands or retain a
parallel command engine after migration.

## Plan-wide execution rules

- [ ] Treat `docs/verified-command-execution-proposal.md` as authoritative for
      command execution, timeout placement, retry, cleanup transactions,
      resource ownership, protocol evidence, migration, and validation cadence.
- [ ] Treat `docs/directed-acyclic-graph-proposal.md` and its separate `.todo`
      as the complete prerequisite graph contract; do not duplicate or extend
      that utility in this plan.
- [ ] Treat `docs/new-command-proposals.md` as authoritative only for managed
      fixture domain semantics; resolve execution-model conflicts in favor of
      the verified-command proposal.
- [ ] Treat `docs/test-runner-service-design.md` as authoritative for admission,
      leases, quarantine, retention, acknowledgement, cursor reads, persistence
      ownership, and the presentation-neutral UI boundary.
- [ ] Keep every new task unchecked until its implementation, proportionate
      evidence, and required follow-up review are complete.
- [ ] Preserve unrelated worktree and index changes; do not stash or commit
      user work.
- [ ] Add language-standard documentation comments for every introduced or
      changed class and method.
- [ ] Keep driver modules independent of `dwarfspec.host`; inject narrow
      capabilities through run assembly.
- [ ] Preserve Busted ownership of test structure, assertions, and behavior
      result classification.
- [ ] Preserve the proposal's non-goal boundaries.
  - [ ] Do not introduce a JavaScript-style command queue, Promise model, DOM
        semantics, fluent-chain retry engine, or a replacement assertion
        framework.
  - [ ] Do not infer product-specific intent from generic input or treat a
        completed render as proof of correct rendered content.
  - [ ] Do not claim arbitrary synchronous Lua/native code can be interrupted;
        require bounded cooperative command and cleanup callbacks.
  - [ ] Do not redesign controller discovery, transport, result persistence,
        TestBed, or the host service beyond the evidence and lifecycle changes
        required by the proposal.
- [ ] Apply the proposal's risk-based validation cadence.
  - [ ] Use syntax/static checks, the relevant conformance case, and a focused
        command suite for a mechanical definition conversion.
  - [ ] Add the smallest relevant live case only for command-specific native
        semantic changes.
  - [ ] Run the complete command-engine suite after runner, deadline, outcome,
        cleanup, or workflow-engine changes.
  - [ ] Run focused protocol, journal, persistence, configuration, host-loading,
        declaration, and formatting checks after schema changes.
  - [ ] Run the full unit suite and representative live qualification at each
        completed command-family boundary, not after each mechanical command.
  - [ ] Reserve full source/package/framework/live qualification for final
        consolidation and other explicitly identified integration gates.

## Phase 0: Complete the standalone DAG prerequisite

Outcome: the generic graph implementation is independently qualified before
resource-claim and cleanup-planning consumers depend on it.

- [ ] Complete every item in `docs/directed-acyclic-graph.todo`.
- [ ] Confirm `src/dwarfspec/graphs/directed_acyclic_graph.lua` exports exactly
      the approved domain-neutral API.
- [ ] Confirm its focused suite and applicable syntax, formatting, and
      declaration checks pass.
- [ ] Record the prerequisite review evidence in this implementation effort.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] The DAG prerequisite is complete and no graph implementation task is
      duplicated in this checklist.

## Phase 1: Inventory the migration and freeze shared vocabulary

Outcome: all existing execution, cleanup, configuration, declaration, and
result surfaces are mapped to the approved contracts before structural changes
begin.

- [ ] Inventory every public command binding in `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`, every
      subject fluent command, and every definition source under
      `src/dwarfspec/driver/commands/`.
  - [ ] Record each command's current signature, return value, mutation status,
        waits, render behavior, cleanup behavior, and direct implementation
        dependencies.
  - [ ] Assign each command exactly one approved `ECommandKind`.
  - [ ] Assign each non-workflow definition an intrinsic-verification policy.
  - [ ] Assign every initial built-in mutator `ONCE`; reserve
        `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE` for the synthetic qualification definition and
        later definitions that separately satisfy the complete proof contract.
  - [ ] Identify commands with existing public `{wait=false}` behavior that
        must retain explicit asynchronous invalidation semantics.
- [ ] Inventory project command loaders and every repository fixture command
      module that currently exports bare callbacks.
- [ ] Inventory existing cleanup registries, cleanup callbacks, lifecycle
      hooks, result fields, quarantine decisions, and `cleanup_confirmed`
      production and consumption sites.
- [ ] Inventory current command-observer events, protocol schemas, controller
      formatting, result persistence, retained-run reads, cursor reads, and
      transport fixtures affected by the coordinated revision.
- [ ] Inventory `settings.wait.timeout_ms`, per-command timeouts, unlimited
      timeout support, and all configuration/declaration examples.
- [ ] Establish a requirement-to-task traceability table or review notes that
      cover every proposal acceptance criterion and every inventoried public
      command.
- [ ] Confirm no compatibility question requires changing an undocumented or
      inconsistent return value; record any such exception as a separate
      decision before editing behavior.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every public command and cleanup/result/configuration touchpoint has an
      assigned migration destination and validation boundary.
- [ ] No command family or project fixture can silently remain on an old path.

## Phase 2: Add protocol enums, settings, and public declaration contracts

Outcome: closed vocabulary, finite timeout configuration, public option types,
and versioned schema shapes exist before runtime components emit them.

- [ ] Add immutable protocol enums for:
  - [ ] `ECommandKind`: `QUERY`, `ASSERTION`, `ACTION`, `STATE_SETTER`,
        `WORKFLOW`, and `FIXTURE`.
  - [ ] `EIntrinsicVerificationKind`: `PRIMARY_OBSERVATION`, `CALLBACK`, and
        `EXECUTION_RECEIPT`.
  - [ ] `EExecutionRetryPolicy`: `ONCE` and `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE`.
  - [ ] `EExecutionOwnerScope`: `SERVICE_RUN`, `SUITE_EXECUTION`, and
        `TEST_ATTEMPT`.
  - [ ] `ECleanupOwnerScope`: `SERVICE_RUN`, `SUITE_EXECUTION`, and
        `TEST_ATTEMPT`.
  - [ ] `ECleanupLifetime`: `OWNER` and `COMMAND`.
  - [ ] Cleanup state, terminal disposition, execution trigger, and command
        failure-stage values required by the proposal.
- [ ] Add `settings.command.timeout_ms` and `settings.cleanup.timeout_ms` with
      positive finite integer validation and 10,000 ms framework defaults.
- [ ] Implement timeout precedence for invocation, project, definition, and
      framework command defaults.
- [ ] Implement cleanup timeout precedence for registration, project, and
      framework cleanup defaults.
- [ ] Introduce the temporary `settings.wait.timeout_ms` fallback only when the
      command setting is absent, with a bounded deprecation diagnostic.
- [ ] Retain legacy `timeout_ms=false` behavior only for the documented
      compatibility window and keep it bounded by the enclosing run lease.
- [ ] Make `settings.command.timeout_ms` win deterministically when both new and
      legacy wait settings are present, emit the legacy-setting deprecation,
      and reject only conflicts whose precedence cannot be applied.
- [ ] Preserve optional frame budgets as secondary safeguards for frame-based
      commands without allowing them to replace the wall-clock deadline.
- [ ] Add declaration types for `CommandOptions`, command observations,
      definitions, workflow definitions and outputs, cleanup registrations and
      transactions, cleanup contexts, claim plans/bindings/registrations/
      references, and all typed outcomes.
- [ ] Add trailing `CommandOptions` overloads without changing logical option
      records or existing positional calls.
- [ ] Define the successor service/event/result schema versions as one
      coordinated protocol revision; do not emit new events under the old
      version.
- [ ] Add structural schema shapes for command ancestry, owner identities,
      cleanup lifecycle events, suite execution records, and all three cleanup
      result projections.
- [ ] Update protocol negotiation to reject every mixed old/new client-service
      combination.
- [ ] Add focused enum, configuration, declaration, protocol-version, and
      schema tests, including invalid finite timeout values and mixed-version
      rejection.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review of the introduced vocabulary against the
      proposal before consumers depend on it.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] All shared enums and public types are immutable, declared, versioned, and
      consistently named.
- [ ] Configuration accepts the compatibility window but cannot create a new
      unbounded command or cleanup contract.
- [ ] Focused protocol/configuration/declaration evidence passes.

## Phase 3: Implement command outcomes, deadlines, definitions, and registry

Outcome: immutable command definitions can be validated and registered, and
all lifecycle outcomes and finite timing calculations have one implementation.

- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/outcomes.lua`.
  - [ ] Implement explicit `ready`, `pending`, `fatal`, and intrinsic-only
        `effect_absent` outcomes.
  - [ ] Implement `executed`, explicit `retry`, and structured `failed`
        execution outcomes.
  - [ ] Keep public results, private verification receipts, effect receipts,
        attempt receipts, and bounded evidence distinct.
  - [ ] Make omitted `effect_receipt` explicitly mean no cleanup-requiring
        effect for every execution outcome.
  - [ ] Reject implicit retry inference from `false`, `nil`, thrown errors,
        timeout, or cancellation.
- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/deadline.lua`.
  - [ ] Use an injected monotonic clock.
  - [ ] Calculate one absolute deadline after successful synchronous
        normalization.
  - [ ] Centralize remaining-time calculation and one documented rounding
        direction.
  - [ ] Preserve evidence and report execution timeout when bounded synchronous
        execution returns after its deadline.
- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/definition.lua`.
  - [ ] Validate names, kinds, normalize/preflight callbacks, execution policy,
        intrinsic policy, timeouts, diagnostics, claims, and cleanup shape.
  - [ ] Require `execute` for every non-workflow definition and require one
        `workflow` while forbidding `execute` for `WORKFLOW`.
  - [ ] Require `operation_key` only for `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE`.
  - [ ] Require `verify` for `CALLBACK`, forbid it for
        `PRIMARY_OBSERVATION`, and omit it for `EXECUTION_RECEIPT`.
  - [ ] Require immutable cleanup policy, callable restore, required cleanup
        verification, supported lifetime, and optional resource projection for
        any definition that can report an effect.
  - [ ] Reject effect-producing shapes without cleanup policy and cleanup
        policies for definitions that cannot produce effects.
  - [ ] Copy or freeze accepted definitions.
- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/registry.lua`.
  - [ ] Merge built-in and project definitions deterministically.
  - [ ] Reject duplicate names and replacement of reserved built-in names.
  - [ ] Preserve source-path and command-name diagnostics for invalid project
        definitions.
- [ ] Add structural unit tests separately from behavioral conformance tests.
  - [ ] Prove all valid kind/intrinsic/retry combinations.
  - [ ] Prove invalid workflow, cleanup, verify, retry, timeout, and reserved
        name combinations fail early.
  - [ ] Prove definitions cannot be mutated after registration.
- [ ] Reserve behavioral qualification for conformance fixtures.
  - [ ] Prove every normalizer and claims projector is bounded, synchronous,
        and non-yielding.
  - [ ] Prove preflight, claims, and verification callbacks remain read-only.
  - [ ] Require each `EXECUTION_RECEIPT` definition to document and test why its
        immutable receipt is the strongest truthful framework evidence.
  - [ ] Prove cleanup resource projection accepts only the effect receipt and
        emits bindings to the previously validated plan.
  - [ ] Prove diagnostic projection is bounded plain data.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review before building the runner on these contracts.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] The registry can accept only structurally coherent immutable definitions.
- [ ] Outcome constructors cannot erase the distinction between command
      evidence and a confirmed cleanup-requiring effect.
- [ ] Deadline behavior is deterministic under a fake clock.

## Phase 4: Implement stage-restricted contexts and diagnostics

Outcome: callbacks receive only the capabilities legal for their lifecycle
stage, and all retained evidence is bounded and serialization-safe.

- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/context.lua`.
  - [ ] Expose monotonic time, remaining deadline, cancellation, stable target
        and mount resolution, read-only claim lookup, render observation,
        bounded diagnostics, and immutable identities through
        `CommandReadContext`.
  - [ ] Add cooperative frame, tick, event, predicate waits, and internal step
        execution only to `CommandExecutionContext`.
  - [ ] Keep cleanup registration, claim mutation, registry mutation, host
        service, controller transport, and result publication inaccessible.
  - [ ] Define a narrow privileged `CleanupRegistrationCapability` used only by
        the runner and the `registerCleanup` definition.
  - [ ] Enforce stage guards for mutable cleanup handles and public command
        invocation.
- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/diagnostics.lua`.
  - [ ] Validate and freeze bounded plain evidence.
  - [ ] Rate-limit or summarize repeated pending observations.
  - [ ] Support command-kind diagnostic providers without serializing
        callbacks, userdata, mutable receipts, or unrestricted arguments.
  - [ ] Record artifact-capture failures without masking the originating
        command failure.
- [ ] Define immutable command identity data: invocation, root, direct parent,
      execution owner, target, cleanup checkpoint, and current stage.
- [ ] Add unit tests proving each context permits its intended capabilities and
      rejects mutations, cleanup registration, or handle execution in forbidden
      stages.
- [ ] Add conformance cases for direct native/global mutation that capability
      objects cannot technically prevent, keeping this behavioral proof
      separate from structural context tests.
- [ ] Add tests for bounded evidence, sanitization, pending aggregation, and
      diagnostic-provider failure.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up capability-boundary review, including driver-to-host
      dependency checks.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] No ordinary definition callback can directly mutate cleanup or resource
      ownership through a supplied context.
- [ ] Diagnostic output is bounded, plain, and safe for journal publication.

## Phase 5: Implement run-wide resource claim policy

Outcome: one `ResourceDependencyIndex` validates inert plans and tracks every
active effect-backed claim across all execution-owner levels.

- [ ] Add the run-scoped `ResourceDependencyIndex` over one qualified
      `DirectedAcyclicGraph` instance.
- [ ] Implement stable run-unique active claim IDs and immutable
      `ResourceClaimReference` values with minimum owner/resource correlation.
- [ ] Expose claim references only through an owning transaction or a stable
      resource-producing command result whose documented domain contract
      explicitly permits downstream dependency or compatible sharing.
- [ ] Reject forged, stale, foreign-run, or policy-incompatible references.
- [ ] Implement inert `ResourceClaimPlanEntry` validation.
  - [ ] Require a nonempty invocation-local unique `claim_key`.
  - [ ] Support exact existing-resource and provisional creation identities.
  - [ ] Derive provisional creation identity from invocation ID and additionally
        the stable operation key for `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE`.
  - [ ] Keep local relationship keys distinct from references to active claims.
  - [ ] Validate complete plans without allocating claim IDs, creating a
        cleanup transaction, publishing events, or changing index state.
- [ ] Implement `ResourceClaimBinding` validation against a previously frozen
      plan.
  - [ ] Permit activation of an explicit subset by `claim_key`.
  - [ ] Bind provisional entries to concrete stable identities from the effect
        receipt.
  - [ ] Reject duplicate/unknown keys, incompatible identities, new
        relationships, and unplanned pre-existing exclusive resources.
- [ ] Implement exact post-effect `ResourceClaimRegistration` validation for
      downstream cleanup registration without provisional identities.
- [ ] Enforce run-wide exclusive conflicts across service, suite, test, and
      command lifetimes rather than registry-local conflicts.
- [ ] Support only explicitly declared compatible sharing, dependency,
      consumption, and transfer contracts.
- [ ] Default consumption to the claim's exact owner unless an explicit command
      contract authorizes sharing, dependency, consumption, or transfer.
- [ ] Enforce parent owner chains and lifetime direction.
  - [ ] Treat command lifetime as nested within its suite/test owner.
  - [ ] Treat test-attempt lifetime as nested within suite execution.
  - [ ] Treat suite-execution lifetime as nested within service run.
  - [ ] Reject sibling or incomparable dependencies unless an explicit transfer
        command has already rehomed ownership.
- [ ] Insert prerequisite-to-dependent claim edges only after all DwarfSpec
      policy checks pass.
- [ ] Prevent releasing a prerequisite while any active dependent remains.
- [ ] Keep fail-closed conflicted claim records as quarantine evidence without
      treating them as normal active graph claims.
- [ ] Keep runtime claim/index state separate from authoritative cleanup
      history and result projection.
- [ ] Add exhaustive focused tests for plan purity, reference validation,
      binding, conflicts, compatible sharing, lifetime direction, cross-level
      lookup, dependency edges, release blocking, stale references, and
      fail-closed conflict evidence.
- [ ] Treat every attempt plan as the conservative possible claim set of one
      prospective transaction even when the effect may bind only a subset; use
      separate workflow steps when this conservative simulation would reject
      otherwise independent effects.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up review against the proposal's complete claim contract.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] No active claim can exist without exactly one registered cleanup
      transaction ID.
- [ ] No inert or no-effect plan appears as active ownership, cleanup history,
      or result data.
- [ ] Conflicts and invalid dependency directions are rejected across the
      complete run.

## Phase 6: Implement cleanup planning and transaction execution

Outcome: executable cleanup transactions have one state machine, deterministic
dependency-safe planning, required verification, and finite execution.

- [ ] Add `CleanupPlanner` using temporary
      `CleanupTransactionDependencyGraph` instances backed by the generic DAG.
  - [ ] Project active claim edges between their owning transactions.
  - [ ] Collapse claim edges within one transaction.
  - [ ] Simulate an inert plan as one graph-local prospective transaction node
        before mutation, without allocating or publishing a transaction.
  - [ ] Reject claim- or transaction-level cycles during simulation.
  - [ ] Revalidate the committed bound subset before linking and before cleanup
        planning.
  - [ ] Return dependent-before-prerequisite reverse-topological order.
  - [ ] Use reverse owner-local registration order only as the tie-breaker for
        independent transactions.
- [ ] Replace bare cleanup callbacks with `CleanupTransaction` handles.
  - [ ] Support `pending`, `running`, `complete`, `failed`, `abandoned`, and
        `unconfirmed` state.
  - [ ] Make `complete`, `failed`, `abandoned`, and `unconfirmed` the exhaustive
        terminal dispositions.
  - [ ] Implement `execute(reason?)`, `isPending()`, and frozen
        `claimReferences()`.
  - [ ] Make repeated execution an idempotent no-op returning `false` after an
        already-expended transaction.
  - [ ] Remove an eligible transaction from the pending registry before its
        restore callback runs.
  - [ ] Keep a dependency-blocked manual transaction pending, publish no start
        event, and allow a later retry after dependents are expended.
  - [ ] Expose no public cancellation/discard operation for an effect-backed
        transaction; require manual expenditure or automatic finalization.
- [ ] Validate, defensively copy, and freeze cleanup receipts as bounded plain
      data; reject callbacks, userdata, cycles, and live pointers.
- [ ] Deliver the same immutable receipt to restore and verification callbacks;
      do not expose mutable transaction state.
- [ ] Implement finite cleanup deadlines and fresh cleanup cancellation scopes.
- [ ] Make restore one-shot, bounded, and nonblocking.
- [ ] Make required cleanup verification read-only and retryable.
  - [ ] Accept truthy results and assertion callbacks that return normally.
  - [ ] Retry `false`, `cleanup.pending`, and thrown assertion/error observations
        under the remaining cleanup deadline.
  - [ ] Fail immediately on `cleanup.fatal`.
  - [ ] Attempt verification after restore throws when time remains, while
        preserving the failed transaction outcome.
- [ ] Restrict cleanup callbacks.
  - [ ] Restore cannot invoke public commands or register cleanup.
  - [ ] Verification may invoke only read-only queries and assertions.
  - [ ] Mutating commands and cleanup registration are fatal contract errors.
- [ ] Implement automatic command, test, suite, and service cleanup through the
      same planner and transaction executor.
- [ ] Continue attempting later eligible transactions after an individual
      failure.
- [ ] Materialize a prerequisite as `failed/dependency_blocked` when a failed
      or unconfirmed dependent makes safe execution impossible.
- [ ] Compose restore, verification, dependency, timeout, and earlier lifecycle
      failures without masking evidence.
- [ ] On manual restore/verification failure, record terminal `failed`, remove
      the transaction from pending work so teardown cannot retry it, then raise
      the composed failure into the currently active Busted lifecycle without
      changing the transaction's tagged owner.
- [ ] Add focused transaction/planner tests for every state transition,
      deadline, cancellation, retry, error-composition, LIFO tie-break,
      dependency-blocked, and callback restriction.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up cleanup-engine review before integrating it with the
      command runner.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every cleanup operation has an independent finite deadline and required
      terminal verification or typed abandonment proof.
- [ ] The planner never releases a prerequisite ahead of an active dependent.
- [ ] Manual and automatic execution use the same state machine and evidence.

## Phase 7: Implement atomic cleanup registration and lifecycle journaling

Outcome: one service atomically creates effect-backed transactions and claims,
and the append-only service journal retains every lifecycle disposition after
pending work is expended.

- [ ] Add one run-scoped internal `CleanupRegistrationService` as the only
      mutation boundary for transaction IDs, owner registries, execution
      indexes, receipt freezing, claim linking, and registration events.
- [ ] Allocate run-unique transaction IDs and owner-local registration ordinals
      before protected state is published.
- [ ] Atomically create a transaction and all active claims from a confirmed
      effect receipt and frozen claim bindings.
- [ ] Preserve the tagged suite/test owner and owning-command correlation on
      command-lifetime transactions; use lifetime to control finalization, not
      to replace execution-owner identity.
- [ ] Revalidate the bound subset against the current resource index at the
      post-effect registration boundary.
- [ ] Serialize each DwarfSpec mutating attempt from final plan validation
      through atomic effect registration, including across cooperative yields,
      while allowing nested read-only commands.
- [ ] On unexpected post-effect conflict or adapter-contract violation:
  - [ ] Preserve the cleanup transaction and receipt.
  - [ ] Record fail-closed conflicted ownership evidence.
  - [ ] Fail the command at cleanup registration.
  - [ ] Block incompatible work and apply executor quarantine until recovery
        establishes actual resource state.
- [ ] Add runner-only `abandonSelfRolledBack(transaction_id, proof)`.
  - [ ] Accept only a pending internal transaction owned by the current
        invocation.
  - [ ] Require bounded typed stable-identity absence proof from
        `command.effect_absent`.
  - [ ] Atomically remove pending work, release claims, record `abandoned`, and
        publish exactly one abandonment event.
  - [ ] Leave the transaction pending for ordinary cleanup when validation or
        atomic transition fails.
- [ ] Extend the service's append-only journal with registered, started,
      finished, and abandoned cleanup transaction events.
- [ ] Include bounded owner, transaction, ordinal, label, lifetime, command,
      trigger, timing, restore, verification, evidence/failure, and disposition
      fields; exclude executable callbacks and private receipts.
- [ ] Guarantee exactly one terminal event per registered transaction retained
      by a surviving service.
- [ ] Terminalize unresolved work as `unconfirmed` only when interruption or a
      recoverable host failure prevents a conclusive normal outcome.
- [ ] Keep pending registries and mutable execution indexes owner-local while
      keeping the physical journal run-scoped and authoritative.
- [ ] Add service tests for atomic registration, post-effect conflicts,
      transaction/claim identity, abandonment fallback, event completeness,
      terminal uniqueness, and retention after registry removal.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up service/journal review before lifecycle integration.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Every active claim and transaction is created as one service mutation.
- [ ] Removing pending work never removes its historical events.
- [ ] The journal alone can reproduce every retained transaction ledger.

## Phase 8: Integrate suite, test, and service cleanup ownership

Outcome: commands and cleanup are legal at every approved lifecycle level, and
each owner closes, finalizes, reports, and quarantines independently.

- [ ] Introduce stable suite-execution identity for each selected spec file in
      each repeat.
- [ ] Activate suite ownership before suite setup and keep it active through
      suite teardown and suite cleanup.
- [ ] Activate a nested test-attempt identity before attempt setup and keep it
      active through body, teardown, and attempt cleanup.
- [ ] Select the most specific active public owner.
  - [ ] Attribute attempt setup/body/teardown work to the test attempt.
  - [ ] Attribute suite setup/teardown work outside an attempt to the suite.
  - [ ] Reject caller-initiated commands and cleanup registration when neither
        suite nor test ownership is active.
  - [ ] Reserve `SERVICE_RUN` commands exclusively for read-only query/assertion
        children of service-owned cleanup verification.
- [ ] Support internal service effects through the same transaction abstraction
      and `SERVICE_RUN` cleanup owner.
- [ ] Close test registration after attempt teardown, execute remaining pending
      work, terminalize unresolved transactions, and materialize results before
      `test.finished`.
- [ ] Close suite registration after suite teardown, execute and terminalize
      remaining suite work, and materialize results before `suite.finished`.
- [ ] Guarantee suite finalization after suite setup failure, skipped teardown,
      zero runnable tests, or interruption.
- [ ] Run service cleanup after all suite and test owners close and materialize
      it before `run.finished`.
- [ ] Apply dependency-safe ordering across owner scopes and retain blocked
      prerequisite evidence when failed/unconfirmed dependents remain.
- [ ] Keep manual execution attributed to the transaction's original owner even
      when a suite transaction is executed from a nested test.
- [ ] Keep behavior status distinct from cleanup outcome while making failed or
      unconfirmed cleanup affect run failure and quarantine.
- [ ] Combine all transaction failures with the existing lifecycle probes before
      setting authoritative `cleanup_confirmed`.
- [ ] Ensure emergency finalizers attempt safe pending work and mark only work
      that remains unresolved as `unconfirmed`.
- [ ] Add lifecycle tests for ownership selection, setup/teardown legality,
      finalization ordering, setup failure, zero tests, skipped teardown,
      interruption, manual cross-context execution, and service cleanup.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up lifecycle review before exposing result projections.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Suite-, test-, and service-owned transactions never leak into another
      owner's registry or result set.
- [ ] `test.finished`, `suite.finished`, and `run.finished` are each published
      only after the relevant cleanup projection is terminal.

## Phase 9: Materialize and validate cleanup results end to end

Outcome: live, retained, controller, persisted, and future UI consumers can
inspect complete owner-specific cleanup ledgers derived from the authoritative
journal.

- [ ] Fold test-attempt transaction events by owner and registration ordinal
      into `host_report.test_attempts[].cleanup_transactions`.
- [ ] Fold suite-execution events into
      `host_report.suite_executions[].cleanup_transactions`.
- [ ] Fold service-run events into `host_report.service_cleanup_transactions`.
- [ ] Include exactly one terminal disposition per registered transaction:
      `complete`, `failed`, `abandoned`, or `unconfirmed`.
- [ ] Preserve parent suite, repeat, stable suite/test identity, behavior
      summary, cleanup outcome, and bounded failure references where applicable.
- [ ] Update cursor reads and retained-run inspection so transaction state can
      be observed during execution and after terminalization.
- [ ] Update controller result interpretation to validate and persist the
      supplied journal and projections without deriving cleanup semantics from
      private runtime state.
- [ ] Update result storage and formatting for all owner scopes.
- [ ] Validate duplicate IDs, owner-local ordinal rules, owner attribution,
      missing terminal dispositions, journal/result outcome consistency, and
      exact projection equality.
- [ ] Preserve acknowledgement semantics: release admission without erasing the
      read-only retained run record.
- [ ] Keep process/service destruction outside the complete-ledger guarantee;
      do not synthesize registrations or dispositions that the service could
      not publish.
- [ ] Keep successful cleanup absent from high-level warning/status surfaces
      while retaining it for explicit cleanup inspectors.
- [ ] Update protocol snapshots, transport fixtures, retained-run fixtures,
      controller tests, persistence tests, and reporting tests together.
- [ ] Run the focused protocol/event-journal/result/controller/configuration/
      host-loading/declaration validation gate.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up cross-boundary review for schema and projection
      equality.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] The service journal is the sole history source of truth.
- [ ] All three terminal projections are complete, owner-correct, and equal to
      their authoritative journal folds.
- [ ] Old clients/services reject the successor protocol instead of partially
      interpreting it.

## Phase 10: Implement the common command runner

Outcome: every supported command lifecycle can execute through one runner with
stage attribution, a shared deadline, effect-driven cleanup, and composed
failure handling.

- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/runner.lua` and construct it once per
      run through `run_assembly` and `dwarfspec.ds`.
- [ ] Implement invocation setup.
  - [ ] Resolve and validate invocation-level options.
  - [ ] Normalize and defensively copy logical arguments synchronously before
        starting the timed lifecycle.
  - [ ] Derive and freeze the stable operation key during normalization for
        `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE` only.
  - [ ] Resolve the effective finite timeout and create one deadline.
  - [ ] Allocate invocation/root/owner identities and publish sanitized start
        evidence.
  - [ ] Mark command and attempt-local cleanup checkpoints.
- [ ] Implement retryable read-only preflight.
  - [ ] Handle ready, pending, fatal, thrown, cancellation, and timeout.
  - [ ] Retain the latest bounded pending reason/evidence.
  - [ ] Re-resolve stable subjects and mounts across yields.
  - [ ] Revalidate volatile target identity immediately before execution and
        return recoverable changes to the same preflight loop.
- [ ] Invoke `claims(context, request, ready)` after successful final target
      validation; validate and freeze the inert plan without changing ownership.
- [ ] Implement primary behavior by command kind.
  - [ ] Poll query/assertion primary observations and preserve successful nil
        distinctly from pending.
  - [ ] Execute `ONCE` mutators no more than once.
  - [ ] Reject every retry result returned by an `ONCE` definition.
  - [ ] Validate every runtime outcome against its frozen definition.
  - [ ] Fail without executing when no deadline time remains before primary
        execution.
- [ ] Register cleanup immediately when an executed, retry, or failed outcome
      conclusively returns an effect receipt.
  - [ ] Perform registration before later fallible work, yield, verification,
        retry, or publication.
  - [ ] Bind only planned claims through the immutable cleanup policy.
  - [ ] Discard the inert plan on every proven no-effect exit without index
        unwind.
  - [ ] Retain available effect evidence and quarantine if effect absence cannot
        be established after an adapter-contract failure.
- [ ] Establish intrinsic evidence exactly by selected policy.
  - [ ] Retain validated primary observation for `PRIMARY_OBSERVATION`.
  - [ ] Poll the read-only intrinsic callback for `CALLBACK`.
  - [ ] Accept only the immutable execution receipt for
        `EXECUTION_RECEIPT`.
  - [ ] Route valid `effect_absent` through runner-only abandonment and fail the
        intended command effect.
- [ ] Implement optional caller verification after intrinsic verification.
  - [ ] Provide a bounded read-only observation containing command name/kind,
        stable target identity, public result, copied receipt, attempt count,
        elapsed/remaining time, and latest intrinsic evidence.
  - [ ] Retry false/nil and thrown observations under the shared remaining
        deadline.
  - [ ] Preserve the last bounded error/traceback on timeout.
  - [ ] Never replace the command's public return or weaken intrinsic checks.
- [ ] Always expend still-pending command-lifetime cleanup at the finally
      boundary, regardless of primary or verification outcome.
- [ ] Compose primary, intrinsic, caller, timeout, cancellation, and
      command-lifetime cleanup failures without replacing earlier failures.
- [ ] Refresh retained subjects only after the complete composed outcome
      remains successful.
- [ ] Publish exactly one terminal command event after command-lifetime cleanup,
      then return the original public result or raise the composed lifecycle
      error into the active Busted owner.
- [ ] Do not drain unrelated owner-lifetime transactions on command failure.
- [ ] Add fake-clock/fake-scheduler runner tests for every lifecycle stage,
      timeout boundary, receipt path, cleanup checkpoint, composed failure, and
      stable return guarantee.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up runner review against the proposal's ordered
      lifecycle before adding workflows and public bindings.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] No command can report success before its selected intrinsic evidence and
      optional caller verification pass.
- [ ] One deadline covers preflight through caller verification without reset.
- [ ] Cleanup remains independently bounded and survives command failure or
      expiry.

## Phase 11: Implement explicit retry-safe execution

Outcome: the uncommon mutation-retry policy is fully available and proven even
though initial built-ins remain `ONCE`.

- [ ] Validate documented stable operation key, idempotency guarantee,
      attempt-receipt policy, effect-receipt policy, and a command-specific
      conformance fixture for every `EXPLICIT_RETRY_SAFE` definition.
- [ ] Accept another attempt only after an explicit `command.retry` result.
- [ ] Retain bounded immutable attempt receipts and correlate every attempt in
      events and diagnostics.
- [ ] Register every reported retry-attempt effect immediately with forced
      command lifetime.
- [ ] Execute and verify every transaction created since the attempt checkpoint
      before another attempt.
- [ ] Terminate retry when prior-attempt cleanup is failed or unconfirmed.
- [ ] Re-run preflight, volatile target validation, and claim projection/
      validation before each later attempt.
- [ ] Reuse the frozen operation key and original absolute command deadline for
      all attempts.
- [ ] Let cleanup use its independent deadline while counting elapsed cleanup
      wall time against the parent command deadline.
- [ ] Add a synthetic retry-safe definition that proves eventual success,
      fatality, cancellation, timeout, yielding, no-effect retry, effect cleanup,
      cleanup failure, and no retry inferred from false/nil/throw.
- [ ] Add operation-key conformance proving stable bounded scalar derivation
      from the same normalized request.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up retry-policy review and confirm no initial built-in
      mutator opts in without its own proof fixture.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] A later attempt can never execute on top of an unverified prior-attempt
      effect.
- [ ] The runner implements both retry policies from the first production
      release of the new architecture.

## Phase 12: Implement workflows and nested read-only commands

Outcome: composite commands and verification queries share parent ownership,
deadline, cancellation, and trace ancestry without creating parallel public
results.

- [ ] Add `src/dwarfspec/driver/command/workflow.lua`.
- [ ] Validate workflow definitions.
  - [ ] Require unique named steps and one bounded synchronous result projector.
  - [ ] Allow `QUERY`, `ASSERTION`, `ACTION`, `STATE_SETTER`, and `FIXTURE`
        steps; reject nested `WORKFLOW` steps.
  - [ ] Apply the same gate, claims, execution result, retry, effect cleanup,
        intrinsic, diagnostics, and context validation as equivalent public
        command kinds.
  - [ ] Require the containing workflow to use `ONCE` plus
        `EXECUTION_RECEIPT` and omit containing-level execute, claims,
        operation-key, verify, and cleanup callbacks.
- [ ] Implement immutable `WorkflowState` and named `WorkflowOutput` values.
  - [ ] Freeze the normalized request and every bounded successful output.
  - [ ] Represent successful nil as `has_value=false` while retaining the step
        key.
  - [ ] Commit no output for retry, fatal, thrown, cancelled, or timed-out
        outcomes.
  - [ ] Require later steps to re-resolve live objects from stable descriptors.
  - [ ] Derive the stable public result through the pure result projector after
        all steps pass.
- [ ] Give workflow steps unique invocation IDs and parent/root/owner/stage
      correlation without independent public command results or deadlines.
- [ ] Permit read-only public queries/assertions from command preflight and
      intrinsic/caller verification.
  - [ ] Inherit the exact parent deadline, cancellation, owner, root, and
        read-only stage.
  - [ ] Allocate a distinct invocation ID and immediate
        `parent_invocation_id`.
  - [ ] Reject mutating public commands from read-only stages.
- [ ] Permit read-only public queries/assertions from cleanup verification.
  - [ ] Inherit the cleanup owner, fresh cleanup cancellation scope, and
        remaining cleanup deadline.
  - [ ] Use `SERVICE_RUN` execution ownership only for service cleanup.
  - [ ] Set only `parent_cleanup_transaction_id` for the direct child and use
        its own invocation ID as root.
  - [ ] Use normal parent-invocation ancestry and preserve that root for deeper
        descendants.
  - [ ] Reject cleanup registration or public mutation.
- [ ] Add ancestry/schema validation rejecting both parent fields, missing or
      foreign cleanup parents, invalid cleanup stages, and inconsistent
      parent/root/owner identity.
- [ ] Add unit tests for workflow state/output/result behavior, nested command
      deadlines and cancellation, duplicate same-named children, cleanup-rooted
      ancestry, service-run verification, and mutation rejection.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up workflow/nesting review.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Composite behavior uses internal steps rather than calling public
      mutators from primary execution.
- [ ] Every nested event belongs to one valid command tree and exact execution
      owner without extending a deadline.

## Phase 13: Route public cleanup registration and project commands

Outcome: downstream users and project command modules use the same verified
runner and cleanup service without a legacy adapter or recursive cleanup.

- [ ] Implement `ds.registerCleanup(registration, command_options)` as a
      privileged public `ACTION` definition.
  - [ ] Preflight the most-specific active suite/test owner and reject closed or
        absent registration scope.
  - [ ] Validate and freeze label, receipt, restore, required verification,
        optional cleanup timeout, and exact post-effect resource registrations.
  - [ ] Use empty pre-execution claim projection because the caller's effect
        already exists.
  - [ ] Execute `ONCE` through `CleanupRegistrationService`.
  - [ ] Verify the returned transaction is pending under the expected owner,
        owns every activated claim, and has a registration event.
  - [ ] Return the caller-visible transaction handle.
  - [ ] Use transaction identity as the command verification receipt and omit
        `effect_receipt` so registration never recursively creates cleanup.
  - [ ] Retain a fail-closed transaction and conflict evidence if semantic
        registration validation fails after receipt acceptance, even when no
        handle reaches the caller.
- [ ] Enforce the stage guard on transaction handles captured by user code.
- [ ] Document receipts as the supported correctness path for cleanup identity
      and scalar baseline data; acknowledge that closure use cannot be
      structurally prohibited without promoting it.
- [ ] Replace project callback modules with immutable command definition tables.
- [ ] Fail configuration loading early with source path and command name for
      every bare callback or invalid definition.
- [ ] Do not implement a legacy callback adapter.
- [ ] Convert all repository-fixture and known consumer project-command modules
      in the same coordinated change as loader enforcement; built-in command
      definitions remain in the dependency-ordered representative and family
      conversion phases below.
- [ ] Document that direct caller mutation followed by `registerCleanup()` is
      not failure-atomic; require a project-defined verified command with a
      structured partial-effect receipt when that mutation-registration window
      matters.
- [ ] Add focused tests for manual registration, exact claims, returned
      references, early expenditure, teardown expenditure, already-expended
      behavior, owner attribution, cleanup failure propagation, recursive
      registration prevention, and invalid/bare project commands.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up public-cleanup/project-command review.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Runner-managed effects and downstream post-effect registrations create
      the same transaction type through the same service.
- [ ] The runtime contains only definition-based project command execution.

## Phase 14: Add command events and reusable conformance suites

Outcome: the engine has exhaustive reusable proof and publishes structured,
stage-aware command traces suitable for controller and planned UI consumers.

- [ ] Expand command observation from start/finish to stage-aware events for
      preflight, attempts, intrinsic verification, caller verification,
